== Voter enrolment and turnout ==

The rolls listed almost 3 million people registered to vote in the election, a record number representing 95.3% of the estimated eligible voting population.<ref>
{{Deprecated archive|url=https://archive.today/20121220074741/http://www.elections.org.nz/news/enrolment-records-set-2008.html|title=Enrolment records set for 2008 General Election}}, press release, Electoral Enrolment Centre, 12 November 2008.
</ref>
In contrast, voter turnout of 79.5% of enrolled voters came in lower than in most previous elections, the second-lowest since 1978 (when a large number of outdated and duplicate enrolments deflated the figure) and third-lowest since 1902.<ref>{{cite web|title=New Zealand general election, 2008 – Official Results|url=http://www.elections.org.nz/news/2008-election-official-results.html|access-date=23 November 2008|publisher=Elections New Zealand|archive-url=https://archive.today/20120630051204/http://www.elections.org.nz/news/2008-election-official-results.html|archive-date=30 June 2012}}</ref><ref>
[http://www.elections.org.nz/record/resultsdata/elections-dates-turnout.html General elections 1853–2005 – dates & turnout] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081017191904/http://www1.elections.org.nz/record/resultsdata/elections-dates-turnout.html |date=17 October 2008 }}, [http://www.elections.org.nz/study/researchers/electoral-compendium-2005.html Electoral Compendium 2005]  {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081205015253/http://www.elections.org.nz/study/researchers/electoral-compendium-2005.html |date=5 December 2008 }}. Electoral Commission, Wellington, 2005.</ref>

<small>Turnout statistics reflect the percentage of those enrolled to vote.</small>

Political scientist Stephen Levine from [[Victoria University of Wellington|Victoria University]] speculated that the low turnout may have resulted from the National Party's large lead over Labour in opinion polls running up to the election.<ref>{{cite news|title=Low voter turnout for 2008 election |url=http://www.radionz.co.nz/news/events/election2008/stories/2008/11/12/12437f8cf1b7 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |publisher=[[Radio NZ]] |date=12 November 2008 |access-date=12 November 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090725083413/http://www.radionz.co.nz/news/events/election2008/stories/2008/11/12/12437f8cf1b7 |archive-date=25 July 2009 }}
</ref>
Māori Party co-leader [[Pita Sharples]] expressed concern that only 55% of those on the Maori roll had voted.<ref name="NZ_Herald_10542640">{{cite news |url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=10542640 |title=Low Maori voter turnout shows lack of trust – Sharples |date=12 November 2008 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |access-date=30 October 2011 |archive-date=24 March 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120324055641/http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=10542640 |url-status=live }}</ref>

== Issues ==

=== Electoral Finance Act ===
{{See also|Electoral Finance Act|2005 New Zealand election funding controversy}}

The ''[[Electoral Finance Act|Electoral Finance Act 2007]]'' passed by the Labour government had a "chilling effect" on political activity in 2008, according to the [[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Electoral Commission blasts EFA – again |url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz-election-2008/news/article.cfm?c_id=1501799&objectid=10535383 |agency=NZPA |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=2 October 2008 |access-date=2 November 2008 |archive-date=23 May 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110523054941/http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz-election-2008/news/article.cfm?c_id=1501799&objectid=10535383 |url-status=live }}</ref>
Some parties attempted to make this an election issue.

=== Economic conditions ===
On 5 August 2008, the [[New Zealand Treasury|Treasury]] announced that the New Zealand economy had entered a [[recession]].<ref name="stuff_recess">{{cite web |url=http://stuff.co.nz/4644005a13.html |archive-url=https://archive.today/20130223061609/http://stuff.co.nz/4644005a13.html |archive-date=23 February 2013 |title=NZ in recession – Treasury |date=5 August 2008 |access-date=5 August 2008 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |location=New Zealand }}</ref>
Economic downturn has led to high-profile job losses, such as the closure of factories in [[Foxton, New Zealand|Foxton]],<ref name="scoop_foxfelt">{{cite web|url=http://www.scoop.co.nz/stories/PO0808/S00015.htm|title=Tears flow at Feltex Foxton|date=1 August 2008|access-date=5 August 2008|publisher=scoop.co.nz|archive-date=18 October 2008|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081018090122/http://www.scoop.co.nz/stories/PO0808/S00015.htm|url-status=live}}</ref>
in west [[Dunedin]]<ref name="nzh_dunedin_meat">
{{cite news|url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/section/3/story.cfm?c_id=3&objectid=10511151 |title=138 jobs axed in latest meatworks closure |date=29 May 2008|access-date=5 August 2008 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]]}}
</ref>
and in southern [[Hawke's Bay Region|Hawke's Bay]].<ref name="oringi_press">{{cite web|url=http://www.scoop.co.nz/stories/PA0805/S00212.htm|title=Oringi meatworks closure|date=13 May 2008|access-date=5 August 2008|publisher=scoop.co.nz|archive-date=13 May 2008|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080513212735/http://www.scoop.co.nz/stories/PA0805/S00212.htm|url-status=live}}</ref>
At the same time, inflation hit an eighteen-year high,<ref name="zb_inflation">{{cite web|url=http://home.nzcity.co.nz/news/article.aspx?id=87661&cat=976&fm=newsarticle,nur|title=Inflation Hits 18-year high|date=13 May 2008|access-date=5 August 2008|publisher=nzity.co.nz|archive-date=23 May 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110523101315/http://home.nzcity.co.nz/news/article.aspx?id=87661&cat=976&fm=newsarticle,nur|url-status=live}}</ref>
