=== Taxation ===

On 6 October, two days before the National Party's scheduled release of details of the tax-cut plan it had over and above the governing coalition's three-stage series of [[tax cut]]s revealed in the [[2008 New Zealand budget|2008 Budget]],<ref name="scoop_ministate">{{cite web|url=http://www.scoop.co.nz/stories/PA0805/S00405.htm|title=Budget 2008 Minister's Statement|date=22 May 2008|access-date=7 October 2008|publisher=scoop.co.nz|archive-date=30 January 2009|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090130064319/http://www.scoop.co.nz/stories/PA0805/S00405.htm|url-status=live}}</ref>
the Government disclosed its full fiscal situation; it showed that it expected to take $3.1 billion less tax in 2009, forcing the government to borrow $5.9 billion in 2009, rising to $7.3 billion by 2013. This implied higher costs for KiwiSaver, Working For Families and the 20 hours subsidised early-childhood plan; and higher numbers of people forced onto benefits by any prospective economic downturn. Over the next fiscal year, Cullen expected GDP to rise by just 0.1%, with median house prices dropping by an estimated 10–15%.<ref name="stuff_cullen_speech">{{cite web |url=http://www.scoop.co.nz/stories/PA0810/S00124.htm |title=Cullen: Pre-election Economic and Fiscal Update 0 |date=6 October 2008 |access-date=7 October 2008 |publisher=scoop.co.nz |archive-date=24 July 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090724232831/http://www.scoop.co.nz/stories/PA0810/S00124.htm |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="nzh_econ_breakdown">{{cite news |url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/politics/news/article.cfm?c_id=280&objectid=10536185 |title=No More Safety in Numbers For Cullen |date=6 October 2008 |access-date=7 October 2008 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |first=Paula |last=Oliver |archive-date=23 May 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110523054706/http://www.nzherald.co.nz/politics/news/article.cfm?c_id=280&objectid=10536185 |url-status=live }}</ref>

John Key responded to the news by describing the numbers as "a bit worse than we had anticipated", and stated "I'm confident we can deliver a programme of tax cuts."<ref name="nzh_nzh_parade">{{cite news|url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz-election-2008/news/article.cfm?c_id=1501799&objectid=10536172|title=Cullen rains on Key's parade|date=6 October 2008|access-date=7 October 2008|work=[[The New Zealand Herald]]|first=Paula|last=Oliver|archive-date=23 May 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110523054836/http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz-election-2008/news/article.cfm?c_id=1501799&objectid=10536172|url-status=live}}</ref>
The same day Helen Clark reiterated her opinion on tax cuts beyond the government's proposal, saying "now is not the time to go out and recklessly borrow to offer tax cuts",<ref name="tvnz_cutsstill">
{{cite web |url=http://www.tvnz.co.nz/view/page/576182/2178708 |title=is there still money for tax cuts? |date=6 October 2008 |access-date=7 October 2008 |publisher=TVNZ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081007034559/http://tvnz.co.nz/view/page/576182/2178708 |archive-date=7 October 2008}}
</ref>
an opinion she had first voiced in early August<ref name="3news_nuts_says_helen">
{{cite web|url=http://3news.co.nz/PMdescribesNatsplansasnuts/tabid/370/articleID/65494/Default.aspx?ArticleID=65494#video|title=PM describes Nats' plan as 'Nuts'|date=3 August 2008|access-date=5 August 2008|publisher=3 News|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110521021756/http://www.3news.co.nz/PMdescribesNatsplansasnuts/tabid/370/articleID/65494/Default.aspx?ArticleID=65494#video|archive-date=21 May 2011}}
</ref>
when the National Party used its annual conference to promise to speed up the implementation of the tax cuts, and to borrow several billion dollars to fund infrastructure projects such as a $1.5 billion broadband plan and a new prison in its first term.<ref name="nerald_nattax">
{{cite news
|url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/section/1/story.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=10525009&ref=rss
 |title=Faster tax cuts, vows National  |date=3 August 2008|access-date=5 August 2008
|work=[[The New Zealand Herald]]
 |first=Anna
 |last=Rushworth}}
</ref>
On 9 October, National released its policy, promising people on the average wage or higher around $47 a week extra in the hand, funded through a combination of cutting contributions to KiwiSaver, eliminating a tax credit for science and development, and changing Working For Families entitlements.<ref name="nerald_nattax_how">{{cite news
