=== Trust ===

On calling the 2008 election, Prime Minister [[Helen Clark]] declared that it would be "about trust", labelling the National Party's recent commitments to preserve Labour Party programmes such as [[KiwiSaver]] and [[Kiwibank]] as "insincere".<ref name="nzh_clark_teiat">{{cite news |url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/politics/news/article.cfm?c_id=280&objectid=10531965&ref=rss |title=Poll all about trust, says Clark |date=13 September 2008 |access-date=25 September 2008 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |first=Audrey |last=Young |archive-date=22 May 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110522221702/http://www.nzherald.co.nz/politics/news/article.cfm?c_id=280&objectid=10531965&ref=rss |url-status=live }}</ref>

Members of the Labour Party accused John Key of lying about his shareholding in [[Tranz Rail]], by not disclosing nearly half of the shares he and his family trusts owned in the company, even though this presented a clear conflict of interest with Key's role as his party's spokesperson on transport, at a time when he asked several questions in the House about the government's plans regarding rail infrastructure.<ref name="tv3_how_many_shares">
{{cite web|url=http://www.3news.co.nz/JohnKeyaccusedoflyingaboutTransrailshares/tabid/419/articleID/72792/Default.aspx?ArticleID=72792|title=John Key accused of lying about Transrail shares|date=23 September 2008|access-date=25 September 2008|publisher=TV3|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110521015218/http://www.3news.co.nz/JohnKeyaccusedoflyingaboutTransrailshares/tabid/419/articleID/72792/Default.aspx?ArticleID=72792|archive-date=21 May 2011}}
</ref>

New Zealand First leader [[Winston Peters]] faced an attack on his party's credibility, first over allegations that his party did not declare a $100,000 donation from millionaire ex-patriate property developer [[Owen Glenn]] to cover Winston Peters' legal costs in a challenge to the result in the seat of [[tauranga (New Zealand electorate)|Tauranga]].  This was referred to a House of Representatives [[Privileges Committee]]. On 22 September, the committee determined that Peters had "provided misleading information" and recommended he be [[censure]]d;<ref name="stuff_wp_censure">{{cite web |url=http://www.stuff.co.nz/4701732a6160.html |title=Committee recommends censuring Peters |date=22 September 2008 |access-date=7 October 2008 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |location=New Zealand |archive-date=25 September 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080925010216/http://www.stuff.co.nz/4701732a6160.html |url-status=live }}</ref> this was done by the House of Representatives in a 62–56 vote two days later.<ref name="stuff_ntzb_censure">{{cite web|url=http://www.newstalkzb.co.nz/newsdetail1.asp?storyID=145006|title=Parliament votes to censure Peters|date=22 September 2008|access-date=7 October 2008|publisher=[[Newstalk ZB]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090725071247/http://www.newstalkzb.co.nz/newsdetail1.asp?storyID=145006|archive-date=25 July 2009}}</ref> The second allegation revolved around the party's failure to declare the use of a secret trust to funnel large donations into New Zealand First's bank account, even though no donations over $10,000 to New Zealand First has been declared, as the law requires. This case was referred to the [[Serious Fraud Office (New Zealand)|Serious Fraud Office]] for further investigation; on 11 October, New Zealand First was cleared of charges that Peters called a "waste of time"<ref name="stuff_nzf_cleared">{{cite web|url=https://www.tntmagazine.com/archive/peters-cleared-but-clark-keeps-his-portfolio/|title=Peters cleared but PM keeps his portfolio |date=12 October 2008|access-date=2 August 2026 |work=TNT Magazine |agency=NZPA |location=New Zealand }}</ref> and on 24 October, New Zealand First was cleared of wrongdoing by the Electoral Commission, which was investigating donations that the party failed to declare.<ref name="onenews_nzf_cleared">{{cite web|url=http://tvnz.co.nz/view/page/576182/2227446|title=Peters buoyed by donations ruling|access-date=24 October 2008|archive-date=20 May 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110520081552/http://tvnz.co.nz/view/page/576182/2227446|url-status=live}}</ref>

== Electorates ==

=== Boundary changes ===

The Representation Commission altered many of the boundaries of New Zealand's parliamentary [[New Zealand electorates|electorates]] following the [[2006 New Zealand census|2006 census]]; the large growth in population between censuses lead to significant boundary changes, particularly in Auckland, the area around Christchurch and the central North Island. In May 2007, the Representation Commission announced the boundary changes<ref name="elect_com">{{cite news|url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/section/1/story.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=10437554|title=Auckland to get an extra seat in Parliament |newspaper=[[The New Zealand Herald]]|date=3 May 2007 |access-date=3 May 2007|archive-date=29 September 2007|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070929120213/http://www.nzherald.co.nz/section/1/story.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=10437554|url-status=live}}</ref>
to take effect for the next general election, with the boundaries finalised in September 2007.

The Commission announced the formation of a new electorate in Greater Auckland, bringing the number of geographical constituencies to 70. The new seat, originally dubbed "[[Howick, New Zealand|Howick]]" (after the Auckland suburb), would have included parts of the existing Pakuranga, Manukau East and Clevedon electorates. After [[Pakuranga (New Zealand electorate)|Pakuranga]] electors made strong objections to the proposed changes (which would have seen the inclusion of the population centres [[Panmure, New Zealand|Panmure]], [[Point England, New Zealand|Point England]] and [[Glen Innes, New Zealand|Glen Innes]] into the electorate) the Commission largely reverted proposed changes to the boundaries of the [[Pakuranga (New Zealand electorate)|Pakuranga electorate]]. The Commission opted to alleviate population pressures by moving the Auckland City suburb of [[Ōtāhuhu]] into [[Manukau East]]. The revised new seat received the name [[Botany (New Zealand electorate)|"Botany"]] to reflect its focus on the growing population-centres of [[Botany Downs, New Zealand|Botany Downs]]–[[Dannemora, New Zealand|Dannemora]]. On paper, Botany counts as a safe National seat.

Even though the number of South Island electorates remains fixed, the decline in the population of electorates south of [[Christchurch]] has resulted in the boundaries of electorates from [[Invercargill (New Zealand electorate)|Invercargill]] north to [[Rakaia (New Zealand electorate)|Rakaia]] shifting northwards. The seats of [[Aoraki (New Zealand electorate)|Aoraki]], [[Otago (New Zealand electorate)|Otago]], [[Rakaia (New Zealand electorate)|Rakaia]] and [[Banks Peninsula (New Zealand electorate)|Banks Peninsula]] all gravitated towards Christchurch. In the process:
* [[Aoraki (New Zealand electorate)|Aoraki]] received the new name of [[Rangitata (New Zealand electorate)|Rangitata]]
* [[Otago (New Zealand electorate)|Otago]] received the new name of [[Waitaki (New Zealand electorate)|Waitaki]]
* [[Rakaia (New Zealand electorate)|Rakaia]] received the new name of [[Selwyn (New Zealand electorate)|Selwyn]]
* [[Banks Peninsula (New Zealand electorate)|Banks Peninsula]] received the new name of [[Port Hills (New Zealand electorate)|Port Hills]]

Other seats in the lower South Island increased dramatically in size.

=== Situation after 2005 ===

In 2005 four MPs won seats with majorities of under a thousand: Labour's [[Darren Hughes]] beat National candidate [[Nathan Guy]] in [[Ōtaki (New Zealand electorate)|Ōtaki]] by 382 votes (1.00%), and in [[Hamilton West (New Zealand electorate)|Hamilton West]], [[Martin Gallagher (politician)|Martin Gallagher]] of the Labour Party won an 825-vote majority (2.46%) over National's Tim Macindoe. Both these seats saw a rematch in 2008, with the National Party candidates emerging victorious in each.

The swing to National in the central North Island saw two Bay of Plenty seats produce close results: in [[Rotorua (New Zealand electorate)|Rotorua]], the sitting Labour MP [[Stephanie Chadwick|Steve Chadwick]] prevailed by just 662 votes (2.17%) over National's Gil Stehbens, and in [[Tauranga (New Zealand electorate)|Tauranga]], property developer [[Bob Clarkson]] defeated New Zealand First's leader and seven-term MP for Tauranga Winston Peters by 730 votes (2.02%). Rotorua fell to National's [[Todd McClay]] in 2008, while [[Simon Bridges]] held Tauranga for National by a wide margin, preventing Peters from returning to Parliament.

Besides the three Labour-held narrow-margin seats mentioned above (Otaki, Hamilton West and Rotorua), National had prospects of gaining [[Taupō (New Zealand electorate)|Taupō]], where boundary changes have added the National-leaning town of [[Cambridge, New Zealand|Cambridge]] and with it nearly 20,000 different voters – putting sitting MP [[Mark Burton (politician)|Mark Burton]]'s 2005 majority of just 1,285 votes (4.43%) at risk. Similarly, the seat of [[West Coast-Tasman (New Zealand electorate)|West Coast-Tasman]] gave Labour's [[Damien O'Connor]] a majority of 2,154 (6.77%). National reversed all of these majorities in 2008 and captured all three seats.

Part of National's core vote comes from provincial centres. In 1990, when Labour lost power, it lost every seat between the southern fringe of the Auckland urban area and Porirua except [[Napier (New Zealand electorate)|Napier]] and [[Palmerston North (New Zealand electorate)|Palmerston North]]; in 2005, National again won several provincial seats off Labour:

# [[East Coast (New Zealand electorate)|East Coast]]
# [[Tukituki (New Zealand electorate)|Tukituki]]
# [[Napier (New Zealand electorate)|Napier]]
# [[Whanganui (New Zealand electorate)|Whanganui]]
# [[Hamilton East (New Zealand electorate)|Hamilton East]]
# [[Otago (New Zealand electorate)|Otago]]
# [[Aoraki (New Zealand electorate)|Aoraki]]

National also won Tauranga off New Zealand First leader Winston Peters in 2005 and the lion's share of the ACT and United parties' core votes{{Citation needed|date=September 2008}} (and in the process gained [[Northcote (New Zealand electorate)|Northcote]] off Labour).

The newly-drawn seat of [[Botany (New Zealand electorate)|Botany]] on Auckland's eastern fringe presented an electoral problem for the Labour Party – on 4 July 2008 a crowd of mostly Asian marchers numbered in the thousands<ref>
{{cite web|url=http://www.stuff.co.nz/auckland/4606304a6016.html|archive-url=https://archive.today/20130223032754/http://www.stuff.co.nz/auckland/4606304a6016.html|archive-date=23 February 2013|work=Eastern Courier|title=Clean, green but not safe|date=5 July 2008}}
</ref> protested against Labour's record on crime and sentencing and a perceived upswing in anti-Asian crime. Because of the large Asian population in the new seat, such trends may have given National candidate and victor [[Pansy Wong]] a possible advantage. Boundary changes have also shaken up the electoral landscape of the South Island.<ref name="elect_com" /> Three new seats – [[Selwyn (New Zealand electorate)|Selwyn]], [[Waitaki (New Zealand electorate)|Waitaki]] and [[Rangitata (New Zealand electorate)|Rangitata]], drawn respectively out of Aoraki, Otago and Rakaia, three National-held seats in 2005, damaged Labour's chances outside of Christchurch and Dunedin.{{Citation needed|date=September 2008}}

On Labour's other flank, the three Māori electorates that it held last time against a strong [[Māori Party]] challenge were in danger of falling as they did in 1996 when New Zealand First broke Labour's sixty-year stranglehold. [[Nanaia Mahuta]] again faced [[Angeline Greensill]] for the new [[Hauraki-Waikato]] seat, and narrowly held it. Māori Affairs Minister [[Parekura Horomia]] also held on by a small margin against veteran broadcaster [[Derek Fox (broadcaster)|Derek Fox]] in [[Ikaroa-Rāwhiti]]. Incumbent [[Mahara Okeroa]], however, was defeated by [[Rahui Katene]] in [[Te Tai Tonga]], giving the Māori Party an additional seat.

The seats of [[Tauranga (New Zealand electorate)|Tauranga]] and [[Epsom (New Zealand electorate)|Epsom]] provided particular resonances: Winston Peters failed to retake the [[Marginal seat|marginal]] Tauranga (and [[Ron Mark]] failed to win the [[Rimutaka (New Zealand electorate)|Rimutaka]] seat), meaning [[New Zealand First]]'s chances of returning to the House depended on winning 5% of the party vote, which they did not accomplish. Similarly, the electoral fortunes of the ACT Party depended very largely on [[Rodney Hide]] retaining Epsom, which he did.

Amongst other parties very aware of the 5% barrier, [[United Future New Zealand|United Future]] appeared more secure in the light of [[Peter Dunne]]'s grip on [[Ōhariu]], which he maintained, though by a narrower margin than previously; and the [[New Zealand Progressive Party|Progressive Party]] retained a very strong hold via [[Jim Anderton]]'s "[[safe seat]]" of [[Wigram (New Zealand electorate)|Wigram]].

The Greens never appeared in danger of slipping below the 5% threshold, although lacking an obvious winnable electorate seat (co-leader [[Jeanette Fitzsimons]] had won [[Coromandel (New Zealand electorate)|Coromandel]] in [[1999 New Zealand general election|1999]], but the electorate returned to National in [[2002 New Zealand general election|2002]]).

=== Seats that changed hands ===
National won nine electorate seats from Labour:
* [[Auckland Central (New Zealand electorate)|Auckland Central]]: [[Nikki Kaye]] defeated sitting Labour MP [[Judith Tizard]], the first time National has won this seat.
* [[Hamilton West (New Zealand electorate)|Hamilton West]]: won by [[Tim Macindoe]] who ousted sitting Labour MP [[Martin Gallagher]], a reversal of the 2005 election when Gallagher narrowly defeated Macindoe.
* [[Maungakiekie (New Zealand electorate)|Maungakiekie]]: [[Sam Lotu-Iiga]] achieved a majority of 1,876 over [[Carol Beaumont]] after incumbent [[Mark Gosche]] retired. Beaumont entered Parliament as a Labour list member.
* [[New Plymouth (New Zealand electorate)|New Plymouth]]: the most marginal electorate, where [[Jonathan Young (politician)|Jonathan Young]] won by 314 votes to oust [[Harry Duynhoven]].
* [[Ōtaki (New Zealand electorate)|Otaki]]: the most marginal 2005 seat fell to [[Nathan Guy]], who defeated incumbent Minister [[Darren Hughes]] by 1,422 votes. Hughes remained in Parliament through the Labour list.
* [[Rotorua (New Zealand electorate)|Rotorua]]: fell to [[Todd McClay]] who defeated sitting Minister [[Steve Chadwick]] by 4,855 votes. Chadwick returned to Parliament through the party list.
* [[Taupō (New Zealand electorate)|Taupo]]: won by [[Louise Upston]], who defeated [[Mark Burton (politician)|Mark Burton]] by nearly 6,000 votes, to reverse a 1,198 majority in [[2005 New Zealand general election|2005]].
* [[Waitakere (New Zealand electorate)|Waitakere]]: [[Paula Bennett]] defeated [[Lynne Pillay]] by just over 600 votes, Pillay returned to Parliament as a List MP. This Waitakere seat differs from that previously held by National's [[Brian Neeson]].{{Citation needed|date=December 2008}}
* [[West Coast-Tasman (New Zealand electorate)|West Coast-Tasman]]: [[Chris Auchinvole]] defeated Cabinet Minister [[Damien O'Connor]].

The [[Māori Party]] also won a seat from Labour.
* [[Te Tai Tonga (New Zealand electorate)|Te Tai Tonga]]: [[Rahui Katene]] won the seat from [[Mahara Okeroa]].

=== New seats won by incumbent MPs ===
*  National's [[Pansy Wong]] became the first Member for the new [[Botany (New Zealand electorate)|Botany]] seat. She had become the first Asian list MP in [[1996 New Zealand general election|1996]], and in 2008 became the first Asian electorate MP.
*  National's [[Paul Hutchison (politician)|Paul Hutchison]], sitting MP for [[Port Waikato (New Zealand electorate)|Port Waikato]], returned to Parliament as Member for {{NZ electorate link|Hunua}} which covers most of his former electorate and parts of the former [[Clevedon (New Zealand electorate)|Clevedon]] electorate.
*  United Future's [[Peter Dunne]], sitting MP for [[Ohariu-Belmont (New Zealand electorate)|Ohariu-Belmont]], returned to Parliament as the new Member for [[Ōhāriu (New Zealand electorate)|Ōhariu]].
*  National's [[Judith Collins]], sitting MP for [[Clevedon (New Zealand electorate)|Clevedon]], returned to Parliament as the new Member for [[Papakura (New Zealand electorate)|Papakura]].
*  Labour's [[Ruth Dyson]], sitting MP for [[Banks Peninsula (New Zealand electorate)|Banks Peninsula]], returned to Parliament as the new Member for [[Port Hills (New Zealand electorate)|Port Hills]].
*  National's [[Jo Goodhew]], sitting MP for [[Aoraki (New Zealand electorate)|Aoraki]], returned to Parliament as the new Member for [[Rangitata (New Zealand electorate)|Rangitata]].
*  National's [[Lindsay Tisch]], sitting MP for {{NZ electorate link|Piako}}, returned to Parliament as the new Member for [[Waikato (New Zealand electorate)|Waikato]], covering most of Piako and rural areas north of Hamilton West.
*  National's [[Jacqui Dean]], sitting MP for [[Otago (New Zealand electorate)|Otago]], returned to Parliament as the new Member for [[Waitaki (New Zealand electorate)|Waitaki]] which takes in most of Dean's former Otago electorate, and Queenstown and Arrowtown.
*  Labour's [[Nanaia Mahuta]], sitting MP for the Maori electorate of [[Tainui (New Zealand electorate)|Tainui]], returned to Parliament as the new Member for [[Hauraki-Waikato (New Zealand electorate)|Hauraki-Waikato]].

=== New MPs in vacated seats ===
A number of seats elected new MPs following the retirement of their sitting Members:
* [[Brendon Burns (politician)|Brendon Burns]] held for Labour [[Christchurch Central]] by just over 800 votes, previously held by retiring MP [[Tim Barnett (politician)|Tim Barnett]].
* [[Clare Curran]] held for Labour [[Dunedin South]] vacated by [[David Benson-Pope]] who did not achieve party reselection.
* [[William Sio]] held for Labour the seat of [[Māngere (New Zealand electorate)|Mangere]], defeating [[Taito Phillip Field]]. Field won the seat in the [[2005 New Zealand general election|2005 general election]], but left the party after being indicted on fraud charges and contested this election as leader of [[New Zealand Pacific Party]].
* [[Iain Lees-Galloway]] held for Labour the seat of [[Palmerston North (New Zealand electorate)|Palmerston North]] following the retirement of sitting MP [[Steve Maharey]].
* [[Chris Hipkins]] held for Labour the seat of [[Rimutaka (New Zealand electorate)|Rimutaka]] following the retirement of sitting MP [[Paul Swain (politician)|Paul Swain]].
* [[Amy Adams (politician)|Amy Adams]] won for National the new seat of [[Selwyn (New Zealand electorate)|Selwyn]], taking in part of the former [[Rakaia (New Zealand electorate)|Rakaia]] electorate. Sitting Rakaia MP [[Brian Connell]] retired from politics.
* [[Simon Bridges]] held for National the seat of [[Tauranga (New Zealand electorate)|Tauranga]] following the retirement of sitting MP [[Bob Clarkson]].
* [[Grant Robertson]] held for Labour the seat of {{NZ electorate link|Wellington Central}} following the retirement of sitting MP [[Marian Hobbs]].

== Opinion polling ==
[[Image:NZ opinion polls 2005-2008 new.png|thumb|Poll results for all political parties that exceeded the 5% [[Mixed-member proportional representation|mixed-member proportional]] (MMP) representation threshold between the 2005 and 2008 elections.]]
{{main|Opinion polling for the 2008 New Zealand general election}}

Having come first in the party vote at the 2005 election by just two percentage points, Labour held a slender lead in aggregate polling through the first half of 2006; a two-point lead in the first half of the year turned into a two-point deficit by May. Polling for a preferred Prime Minister showed Helen Clark nearly twice as popular as then National Party leader [[Don Brash]].

Things changed in early 2007, with new National leader [[John Key]] improving on Brash's preferred Prime Minister rating by ten points, and overtaking Clark as preferred Prime Minister in May; at the same time National jumped out to a sizeable lead over Labour ranging from between eight and eighteen points, spending most of 2007 and 2008 with support from around fifty percent of the electorate. Labour's popularity slumped, hitting its lowest point in the winter of 2008, before beginning a slow climb into the high thirties in August and September.

Leading up to the election, polls indicated a range of possible outcomes on election day; some suggested Labour could form a coalition government, while others predicted National in control. Of the "minor" parties, only the Green Party consistently polled over the five-percent threshold, and [[United Future New Zealand|United Future]] and the Progressive Party frequently failed to register a mention. Both ACT's and the [[Māori Party]]'s popularity since 2005 remained steady at around two percent, while New Zealand First failed to poll over the threshold after December 2006. The polls gave varied results for preferred Prime Minister, with some giving Clark a slight lead, and others giving Key a sturdy margin.

== Coalition preferences during the campaign ==
The coalition preferences of various parties played a role during the campaign, due to the likelihood that no party would get an absolute majority of seats in the House. ACT emerged as the first "minor" party to announce that it would support a prospective National-led government.<ref name="act_rodneyspeech">{{cite web|url=http://www.act.org.nz/news/be-the-difference-party-vote-act-0|title=Be The Difference – Party Vote ACT|date=12 October 2008|access-date=14 October 2008|publisher=ACT New Zealand|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081014040836/http://www.act.org.nz/news/be-the-difference-party-vote-act-0|archive-date=14 October 2008}}</ref> [[United Future New Zealand|United Future]] also announced that it would side with National in late October, after supporting the Labour government for six years.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz-election-2008/news/article.cfm?c_id=1501799&objectid=10539556&pnum=0 |title=Dunne cites Greens as reason for backing Key |first=Paula |last=Oliver |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=27 October 2008 |access-date=2 November 2008 |archive-date=23 May 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110523055355/http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz-election-2008/news/article.cfm?c_id=1501799&objectid=10539556&pnum=0 |url-status=live }}</ref>

The Progressive Party, led by [[Jim Anderton]], had served as a steady coalition partner to Labour and the electorate probably expected it to remain so. The Green Party, which abstained from opposing the Labour-led government in supply and confidence votes through the life of the [[48th New Zealand Parliament|48th Parliament]] (2005 to 2008), said on 20 October that the only party of the two main parties it could form a coalition with was Labour.<ref name="rnznews_gp_coalition">{{cite web|url=http://www.radionz.co.nz/news/events/election2008/stories/2008/10/20/12437a1236df |title=Greens rule out coalition with National |date=20 October 2008 |access-date=24 October 2008 |publisher=[[Radio New Zealand]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081021025052/http://www.radionz.co.nz/news/events/election2008/stories/2008/10/20/12437a1236df |archive-date=21 October 2008 }}
</ref> In the light of New Zealand First's run-in with the Serious Fraud Office, John Key ruled out that party as a government support partner on 31 August 2008, saying "the sheer weight of allegations and the actions of Mr Peters in the last few months means that I have lost that confidence in him".<ref name="nzbr_key_nopeters">
{{cite web|url=http://www.nbr.co.nz/article/nats-wont-shift-position-peters-key-34647|title=Nats won't shift position on Peters – Key|date=31 August 2008|access-date=14 October 2008|publisher=[[National Business Review]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110611051152/http://www.nbr.co.nz/article/nats-wont-shift-position-peters-key-34647|archive-date=11 June 2011}}
</ref>
At that time, Peters' future seemed under a cloud; after his party being cleared of charges of serious fraud, National restated its position, saying that the result of the case has not altered it.<ref name="rrnz_petersclear">{{cite web|url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/86403/peters-won't-get-portfolios-back,-despite-sfo-finding |title=Peters won't get portfolios back, despite SFO finding |date=10 October 2008 |access-date=14 October 2008 |publisher=[[Radio New Zealand]] }}</ref>

Based on polls commissioned by the Māori news show ''Marae'',<ref name="maraesite">{{cite web|url=http://tvnz.co.nz/view/tvone_minisite_index_skin/tvone_pooti08_group?oiu|title=Marae – election 08|date=12 October 2008|access-date=14 October 2008|publisher=TVNZ|archive-date=7 December 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211207004917/https://www.tvnz.co.nz/view/tvone_minisite_index_skin/tvone_pooti08_group|url-status=live}}</ref> the [[Māori Party]] appeared likely to win most of the Māori electorates and stood a chance of holding the balance of power. The party's MP for [[Te Tai Tokerau (New Zealand electorate)|Te Tai Tokerau]], [[Hone Harawira]], stated at the end of September that the party could work with both Labour and National.<ref name="stuff_eitheror">{{cite web|url=http://www.stuff.co.nz/4700554a6160.html|title=Māori Party could work with Nats or Labour – Harawira|date=21 September 2008|access-date=14 October 2008|publisher=TVNZ|archive-date=24 September 2008|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080924074744/http://www.stuff.co.nz/4700554a6160.html|url-status=live}}</ref> On 28 September, National announced a commitment to abolish the Māori electorates in time for the 2014 election.<ref name="nzh_mseats">{{cite news|url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=10534713&pnum=0|title=National to dump Maori seats in 2014|date=29 September 2008|access-date=14 October 2008|work=[[The New Zealand Herald]]|first=Yvonne|last=Tahana|archive-date=23 May 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110523055420/http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=10534713&pnum=0|url-status=live}}</ref>
The Māori Party has benefited greatly from the Māori electorates, and its co-leader [[Tariana Turia]] was unimpressed: "They think again that they can deny us the right to participate. If they want a relationship with the Māori Party then very clearly they're starting off on the wrong foot".<ref name="nzh_mseats" /> ''Marae'' polls released on 12 October showed 62 percent of voters polled in the two northernmost Māori electorates were resistant to the idea of a National–Māori government; co-leader Pita Sharples responded to the poll results by saying his party would be "stupid" to ignore the poll figures.<ref name="stuff_marae_62poll">{{cite news |title=Maori voters prefer Labour to Nats - poll |url=https://www.odt.co.nz/news/national/maori-voters-prefer-labour-to-nats-poll-brdm9axh |access-date=1 August 2026 |work=Otago Daily Times |date=15 October 2008 |language=en}}</ref>

